00:00:50.199 --> 00:00:51.705
- Yes I get up at crazy hours,

00:00:51.705 --> 00:00:53.369
whether it's 3:00 in
the morning by the way,

00:00:53.369 --> 00:00:55.123
when we were talking in London
I was getting up at three

00:00:55.123 --> 00:00:58.326
because I needed to be on set
by four, I'm sorry by seven.

00:00:58.326 --> 00:01:01.116
So, whatever time my call time is,

00:01:01.116 --> 00:01:04.726
and I tell this to studios
and directors, and producers,

00:01:04.726 --> 00:01:07.530
so my call time is at seven.

00:01:07.530 --> 00:01:10.455
Then you back your clock up four hours

00:01:10.455 --> 00:01:13.031
and then that's when I
get up and train twice,

00:01:13.031 --> 00:01:14.261
so I do my cardio in then breakfast,

00:01:14.261 --> 00:01:15.619
then I'll go hit the weights.

00:01:15.619 --> 00:01:16.993
Clangin' and bangin'

00:01:16.993 --> 00:01:18.065
we call it.

00:01:18.065 --> 00:01:21.180
Jackin' iron (laughs)

00:01:21.180 --> 00:01:22.274
- But like what time do
you go to bed I mean,

00:01:22.274 --> 00:01:23.714
you might shoot really late at night.

00:01:23.714 --> 00:01:24.879
- I'm averaging,

00:01:24.879 --> 00:01:27.784
on a movie I'll average probably
about five hours of sleep.

00:01:27.784 --> 00:01:29.114
- You know hard work pays.

00:01:29.114 --> 00:01:30.490
I put in a hell of a lot of work

00:01:30.490 --> 00:01:32.618
all throughout this build up in the fight.

00:01:32.618 --> 00:01:33.754
I put out more content than anybody,

00:01:33.754 --> 00:01:36.802
more content than Fox,
more content than ESPN,

00:01:36.802 --> 00:01:38.772
more content than BT Sport,

00:01:38.772 --> 00:01:40.659
more content than
everybody on the (mumble)

00:01:40.659 --> 00:01:41.810
on my own channels.

00:01:41.810 --> 00:01:43.817
You know what I mean I'm a workhorse

00:01:43.817 --> 00:01:45.622
from all angles, not just in the gym.

00:01:45.622 --> 00:01:47.630
I'm building this media empire now myself

00:01:47.630 --> 00:01:48.994
and we pumped out the content.

00:01:48.994 --> 00:01:50.234
I'm very proud of how it went.

00:01:50.234 --> 00:01:52.718
We consistently, daily
movies I was releasing.

00:01:52.718 --> 00:01:56.506
You know from like a month
ago every single day.

00:01:56.506 --> 00:01:58.778
You know what I mean nobody's doing that.

00:01:58.778 --> 00:02:00.261
So, I'm very proud of that.

00:02:00.261 --> 00:02:01.771
And the work pays.

00:02:01.771 --> 00:02:03.491
And hard works pays.

00:02:03.491 --> 00:02:06.970
I put in the work and that's
why I'm sitting at the top.

00:02:06.970 --> 00:02:08.986
I put it all on the line, I show up,

00:02:08.986 --> 00:02:12.466
I perform and I get the results from that.

00:02:12.466 --> 00:02:15.187
- I knew I was wired to
be excited about business.

00:02:15.187 --> 00:02:17.088
How or why, I don't know.

00:02:17.088 --> 00:02:19.930
And there are certain
guys that got the genetics

00:02:19.930 --> 00:02:21.382
to jump out at the gym right?

00:02:21.382 --> 00:02:22.333
- Mhm, mhm.

00:02:22.333 --> 00:02:24.024
- There's certain guys that when they golf

00:02:24.024 --> 00:02:26.479
they have the muscle
memory and the discipline.

00:02:26.479 --> 00:02:30.297
Dirk Nowitzski may not be the
most talented guy in the NBA,

00:02:30.297 --> 00:02:32.146
but his discipline and his focus

00:02:32.146 --> 00:02:33.530
to do what's necessary to be successful,

00:02:33.530 --> 00:02:35.025
he's willing to do,

00:02:35.025 --> 00:02:38.156
combine it with being seven
feet tall and being skilled,

00:02:38.156 --> 00:02:41.194
makes him an amazing basketball player.

00:02:41.194 --> 00:02:43.994
So it's understanding
what your skillset is,

00:02:43.994 --> 00:02:46.778
finding the right place
to use those skills.

00:02:46.778 --> 00:02:48.733
And then going for it.

00:02:48.733 --> 00:02:50.632
Will that make you 250 grand?

00:02:50.632 --> 00:02:52.140
Depends if you pick the right industry.

00:02:52.140 --> 00:02:55.389
But whatever industry you
pick, if you outwork everybody,

00:02:55.389 --> 00:02:58.283
if you try to be a little
smarter than everybody,

00:02:58.283 --> 00:03:00.592
if you try to be a better
salesperson than everybody,

00:03:00.592 --> 00:03:03.048
if you try to be better
prepared than everybody,

00:03:03.048 --> 00:03:04.701
you got your best chance.

00:03:04.701 --> 00:03:07.988
Because it you don't do
it and somebody else does,

00:03:07.988 --> 00:03:09.625
you know I have this saying,

00:03:09.625 --> 00:03:11.791
"Work like someone's trying
to take it all away from you."

00:03:11.791 --> 00:03:12.783
- Mhm.

00:03:12.783 --> 00:03:14.395
- Work like somebody's spending 24 hours

00:03:14.395 --> 00:03:16.217
trying to take it all away from you.

00:03:16.217 --> 00:03:18.277
And that's kind of the way I look at it.

00:03:18.277 --> 00:03:20.360
I remember asking my dad,

00:03:21.401 --> 00:03:23.824
I wanted new basketball shoes

00:03:23.824 --> 00:03:25.488
cause I was a basketball junkie back then.

00:03:25.488 --> 00:03:26.938
He's like, "Well, your shoes work,

00:03:26.938 --> 00:03:28.220
"if you want new pair of tennis shoes,

00:03:28.220 --> 00:03:30.172
"you have to go out there and get a job."

00:03:30.172 --> 00:03:32.461
And I'm like, "Dad, I'm 12 years old."

00:03:32.461 --> 00:03:33.449
And it just so happens

00:03:33.449 --> 00:03:35.129
he was playing poker with his buddies,

00:03:35.129 --> 00:03:38.229
and one of his buddies was
like, "Well I got a job for you,

00:03:38.229 --> 00:03:40.237
"I got these garbage bags we distribute,

00:03:40.237 --> 00:03:41.796
"you could sell them door to door."

00:03:41.796 --> 00:03:42.826
I'm like okay.

00:03:42.826 --> 00:03:46.046
And it was when I was
selling them I realized that

00:03:46.046 --> 00:03:49.098
I liked to sell, and that I could sell.

00:03:49.098 --> 00:03:52.726
And that I recognized
that selling was about

00:03:52.726 --> 00:03:55.346
providing a service and
creating value for people

00:03:55.346 --> 00:03:58.734
that I knew I would, literally back then,

00:03:58.734 --> 00:04:01.234
I knew I could always succeed.

00:04:02.323 --> 00:04:06.492
I was 16 I think, when I
started a stamp company,

00:04:06.492 --> 00:04:10.148
and started going to stamp
shows and trade shows.

00:04:10.148 --> 00:04:12.752
Just working a little bit
harder than other people

00:04:12.752 --> 00:04:15.404
and trading up from one stamp to the next.

00:04:15.404 --> 00:04:17.268
I remember one time I
started with a quarter,

00:04:17.268 --> 00:04:19.460
and bought a stamp and left with $50.

00:04:19.460 --> 00:04:23.105
Thinking, hey if I could do
this I could do anything.

00:04:23.105 --> 00:04:25.652
And it's not like everything
worked, I failed a lot,

00:04:25.652 --> 00:04:28.346
but I never ever felt like

00:04:28.346 --> 00:04:31.593
I wouldn't be able to work
hard enough to succeed.

00:04:31.593 --> 00:04:33.829
I say the one thing my father gave me,

00:04:33.829 --> 00:04:35.044
which was really key,

00:04:35.044 --> 00:04:36.391
was that work ethic.

00:04:36.391 --> 00:04:38.724
That ability to get
myself up in the morning

00:04:38.724 --> 00:04:40.139
no matter how tired I was.

00:04:40.139 --> 00:04:44.271
To push myself through
those pain barriers.

00:04:44.271 --> 00:04:47.163
And again the combination

00:04:47.163 --> 00:04:49.104
of a loss of a mother at six,

00:04:49.104 --> 00:04:51.865
and then being sent out to work at ten.

00:04:51.865 --> 00:04:53.899
I mean starting work
at 5:00 in the morning

00:04:53.899 --> 00:04:55.219
through the milk route,

00:04:55.219 --> 00:04:58.169
being dropped off at 8:45
at school in the milk van.

00:04:58.169 --> 00:04:59.961
That work ethic.

00:04:59.961 --> 00:05:01.996
So that combination of those two,

00:05:01.996 --> 00:05:04.902
work ethic and great insecurity,

00:05:04.902 --> 00:05:07.437
was what got me to where I went to.

00:05:07.437 --> 00:05:10.432
- You tend to be incredibly
modest in terms of

00:05:10.432 --> 00:05:12.432
describing your talents,

00:05:14.083 --> 00:05:16.512
and you always seem to belittle

00:05:16.512 --> 00:05:18.240
your abilities to a certain extent,

00:05:18.240 --> 00:05:21.240
when clearly there's a reason why this,

00:05:21.240 --> 00:05:23.405
everything you do has been working out.

00:05:23.405 --> 00:05:26.718
I was reading about how you
said that to a certain extent,

00:05:26.718 --> 00:05:29.148
that comes from your punk rock roots.

00:05:29.148 --> 00:05:30.812
- Yeah, there's no other choice.

00:05:30.812 --> 00:05:33.786
In those days there's no
idea of, "this makes money".

00:05:33.786 --> 00:05:36.002
There's no idea of "this is my future".

00:05:36.002 --> 00:05:38.329
It was like a week at a
time, we had five shows,

00:05:38.329 --> 00:05:40.691
we're going to get to
Sunday, (exhales sharply)

00:05:40.691 --> 00:05:43.966
And even those days were
like fought with turbulence.

00:05:43.966 --> 00:05:46.607
And so I never really saw a future,

00:05:46.607 --> 00:05:49.368
none of us did, and some of
us, you know, didn't make it.

00:05:49.368 --> 00:05:52.283
Some of them are dead,
and some of them are just,

00:05:52.283 --> 00:05:55.110
kind of tryna recreate their past.

00:05:55.110 --> 00:05:59.866
And so I realized that I
have to be very hardworking,

00:05:59.866 --> 00:06:02.481
because I don't have kind
of the ease of talent.

00:06:02.481 --> 00:06:04.394
And I come from that,

00:06:04.394 --> 00:06:06.798
in a lot of ways it served me very well.

00:06:06.798 --> 00:06:10.251
In that I am kind of as hard
working as I've ever been,

00:06:10.251 --> 00:06:13.348
completely without confidence.

00:06:13.348 --> 00:06:15.499
I have none, I don't want any.

00:06:15.499 --> 00:06:18.031
Cause I think you turn
your back on the thing

00:06:18.031 --> 00:06:19.476
and that's when you get gored.

00:06:19.476 --> 00:06:21.019
It's when you think, "I got this".

00:06:21.019 --> 00:06:22.522
I never think "I got this",

00:06:22.522 --> 00:06:25.245
I think "Oh man, don't screw this up."

00:06:25.245 --> 00:06:26.936
And I do it everything,

00:06:26.936 --> 00:06:30.234
like including you and I
sitting here right now,

00:06:30.234 --> 00:06:33.210
I'm trying to be fully engaged.

00:06:33.210 --> 00:06:37.331
And that intensity I think
has served me quite well.

00:06:37.331 --> 00:06:39.640
But it comes from punk rock absolutely.

00:06:39.640 --> 00:06:41.988
And the last boss I ever had
was my boss at Haagen Dasz,

00:06:41.988 --> 00:06:43.353
my ice cream shop.

00:06:43.353 --> 00:06:45.847
And to this day he
still comes to my shows.

00:06:45.847 --> 00:06:48.620
And whenever I see Steve, who
got me my first apartment,

00:06:48.620 --> 00:06:51.277
I was living in my car, he believed in me.

00:06:51.277 --> 00:06:52.994
Whenever I see him I get all weepy.

00:06:52.994 --> 00:06:55.329
"It's good to see you sir."

00:06:55.329 --> 00:06:56.888
"Henry you can call me Steve."

00:06:56.888 --> 00:06:58.012
"I can't do that Sir."

00:06:58.012 --> 00:07:00.662
Cause he trusted me with his money,

00:07:00.662 --> 00:07:02.790
and I said let me run your store,

00:07:02.790 --> 00:07:04.615
I know how to do this, I can do this.

00:07:04.615 --> 00:07:06.528
He's like, "Well, if you
screw up you're outa here."

00:07:06.528 --> 00:07:07.557
I said, "I won't screw up."

00:07:07.557 --> 00:07:09.539
And it ended up being, I ran a store.

00:07:09.539 --> 00:07:12.431
And so, but it was anger that said,

00:07:12.431 --> 00:07:13.660
"I'll be here all damn week,

00:07:13.660 --> 00:07:14.875
"I'll get this whole thing right."

00:07:14.875 --> 00:07:16.976
I fired a staff and
rehired a bunch of people

00:07:16.976 --> 00:07:18.851
who could really work.

00:07:18.851 --> 00:07:21.211
(mumbling)

00:07:21.211 --> 00:07:22.044
And, out of anger.

00:07:22.044 --> 00:07:23.768
Like I'll be back in four hours

00:07:23.768 --> 00:07:27.730
to run this $3.75 an
hour job into the ground.

00:07:27.730 --> 00:07:29.405
And then I joined Black Flag,

00:07:29.405 --> 00:07:31.956
and I thought I was a hardworking person.

00:07:31.956 --> 00:07:33.356
Then you meet the guys in Black Flag,

00:07:33.356 --> 00:07:34.703
who are so driven.

00:07:34.703 --> 00:07:37.461
You see Greg Ginn work 23 hour days.

00:07:37.461 --> 00:07:39.046
Like Greg you're still on the phone.

00:07:39.046 --> 00:07:39.879
"I know."

00:07:39.879 --> 00:07:40.830
Greg, when did you eat?

00:07:40.830 --> 00:07:41.663
"I don't know."

00:07:41.663 --> 00:07:43.789
Greg, when was the last time you showered?

00:07:43.789 --> 00:07:45.197
"Uh, do we have a shower?"

00:07:45.197 --> 00:07:47.513
Like we were not going to be stopped.

00:07:47.513 --> 00:07:50.550
And I kind of go with that intensity

00:07:50.550 --> 00:07:53.256
in kind of anything I do.

00:07:53.256 --> 00:07:54.300
Like you're very calm,

00:07:54.300 --> 00:07:55.210
good looking young man.

00:07:55.210 --> 00:07:56.686
Look at me I'm a spaz sitting next to you,

00:07:56.686 --> 00:08:00.359
like I'm doing an interview with you!

00:08:00.359 --> 00:08:01.885
- [Interviewer] You're focused.

00:08:01.885 --> 00:08:02.926
- I'm sweating man.

00:08:02.926 --> 00:08:04.500
(audience laughs)

00:08:04.500 --> 00:08:07.696
But I'm like this going
to the airport, like...

00:08:07.696 --> 00:08:09.160
(audience laughs)

00:08:09.160 --> 00:08:12.046
Ten miles out (laughs)

00:08:12.046 --> 00:08:16.179
And so, but it's anger that
is informed kind of my life,

00:08:16.179 --> 00:08:19.509
and unflatteringly a sense of vengeance.

00:08:19.509 --> 00:08:22.388
Every damn person who said
I wouldn't be anything,

00:08:22.388 --> 00:08:24.579
I'm crushing them everyday.

00:08:24.579 --> 00:08:28.003
Everybody I had to endure
in any band I was in,

00:08:28.003 --> 00:08:30.420
everyday into a powder, yeah.

00:08:31.599 --> 00:08:32.719
(audience applause)

00:08:32.719 --> 00:08:33.987
Thank you.

00:08:33.987 --> 00:08:35.651
And I wish them no ill,

00:08:35.651 --> 00:08:37.896
I just wish to shine brighter,

00:08:37.896 --> 00:08:39.915
and if it burns my body to a crisp,

00:08:39.915 --> 00:08:42.427
I'm happy to go right now.

00:08:42.427 --> 00:08:43.917
- Go hard.

00:08:43.917 --> 00:08:46.688
And then when you think
you going hard, go harder.

00:08:46.688 --> 00:08:48.633
Like, that's really it, just hustle.

00:08:48.633 --> 00:08:50.481
There's nothing you can't

00:08:50.481 --> 00:08:53.626
put enough work in every single day,

00:08:53.626 --> 00:08:55.459
and not get better at.

00:08:56.461 --> 00:08:59.294
If you want to be good at something,

00:09:00.883 --> 00:09:04.243
you can't just do it
everyday and not get better.

00:09:04.243 --> 00:09:05.403
It's the same school with music,

00:09:05.403 --> 00:09:06.492
it's the same learning how to play

00:09:06.492 --> 00:09:08.069
an instrument or something.

00:09:08.069 --> 00:09:09.729
If you spend your time practicing a craft,

00:09:09.729 --> 00:09:12.180
like you know, that's really it.

00:09:12.180 --> 00:09:14.087
It just you know,

00:09:14.087 --> 00:09:18.893
cause nowadays with the internet
everything's so accessible,

00:09:18.893 --> 00:09:22.360
everybody in the world is a rapper.

00:09:22.360 --> 00:09:24.237
Everybody's putting music out,

00:09:24.237 --> 00:09:25.504
so it's like how do you stand out?

00:09:25.504 --> 00:09:27.706
Quality's always going to matter,

00:09:27.706 --> 00:09:29.331
but also being different.

00:09:29.331 --> 00:09:30.455
You can't try to be different,

00:09:30.455 --> 00:09:32.357
you just got to be yourself.

00:09:32.357 --> 00:09:34.354
- How is it different today,

00:09:34.354 --> 00:09:37.153
in doing your show, late night,

00:09:37.153 --> 00:09:38.934
than it was ten years ago?

00:09:38.934 --> 00:09:42.989
Are you more instinctive, are you more...

00:09:42.989 --> 00:09:45.312
Do you think less about
it and you just do it?

00:09:45.312 --> 00:09:46.529
- Yeah, It's muscle memory.

00:09:46.529 --> 00:09:47.415
It used to,

00:09:47.415 --> 00:09:48.556
- [Interviewer] Like a golf swing.

00:09:48.556 --> 00:09:49.713
You crunch down your,

00:09:49.713 --> 00:09:53.030
and you're constantly trying to learn.

00:09:53.030 --> 00:09:54.647
You're still thinking,

00:09:54.647 --> 00:09:57.462
but your ratio of thinking
to acting is very different.

00:09:57.462 --> 00:09:58.749
At the beginning

00:09:58.749 --> 00:10:01.847
I was thinking about it
this much in, you know,

00:10:01.847 --> 00:10:04.130
in my cognitive brain.

00:10:04.130 --> 00:10:05.820
And then there was this much,

00:10:05.820 --> 00:10:09.649
whatever thin reed of
talent you have is there,

00:10:09.649 --> 00:10:10.893
and then you're thinking about the rest.

00:10:10.893 --> 00:10:12.252
And then over the years I think,

00:10:12.252 --> 00:10:14.643
you know they always
say the reptile brain.

00:10:14.643 --> 00:10:16.650
The reptile portion of your brain is where

00:10:16.650 --> 00:10:18.538
respiration, heartbeat.

00:10:18.538 --> 00:10:21.009
That's the stuff that even
if you're knocked unconscious

00:10:21.009 --> 00:10:22.368
it's still working.

00:10:22.368 --> 00:10:25.483
I really believe a lot of my
talk show or comedy instincts

00:10:25.483 --> 00:10:27.280
are now in my reptile brain.

00:10:27.280 --> 00:10:28.669
That you could knock me out,

00:10:28.669 --> 00:10:32.842
and my heart would still
beat, I'd still breathe,

00:10:32.842 --> 00:10:34.717
and I'd still be asking Lindsay Lohan,

00:10:34.717 --> 00:10:35.732
(laughing)

00:10:35.732 --> 00:10:36.789
some stupid question.

00:10:36.789 --> 00:10:38.098
- [Interviewer] You have just become,

00:10:38.098 --> 00:10:39.707
some ways it has become part of your DNA.

00:10:39.707 --> 00:10:42.536
- Yeah I mean I think a lot of it is,

00:10:42.536 --> 00:10:44.784
you have to have some ability.

00:10:44.784 --> 00:10:46.549
But then it's just how much will.

00:10:46.549 --> 00:10:47.909
I really do think there's just a,

00:10:47.909 --> 00:10:49.403
I think people underestimate.

00:10:49.403 --> 00:10:50.814
It doesn't sound sexy,

00:10:50.814 --> 00:10:52.335
it doesn't sound cool.

00:10:52.335 --> 00:10:53.482
- [Interviewer] How much do you want it?

00:10:53.482 --> 00:10:54.527
- [Conan] Work ethic.

00:10:54.527 --> 00:10:57.494
And just, how badly do you want it.

00:10:57.494 --> 00:11:00.057
And there've been you know,

00:11:00.057 --> 00:11:00.974
many times,

00:11:02.160 --> 00:11:04.183
throughout the years of doing
my show where I've thought,

00:11:04.183 --> 00:11:06.957
you could shoot me in the
chest before I walk out there,

00:11:06.957 --> 00:11:08.832
and I'd still walk out
there and do that show,

00:11:08.832 --> 00:11:11.247
and then I'll come backstage and die.

00:11:11.247 --> 00:11:12.581
But we're doing that show.

00:11:12.581 --> 00:11:15.129
- [Interviewer] The interesting
thing for me to talk to you,

00:11:15.129 --> 00:11:16.609
and what makes you so fascinating

00:11:16.609 --> 00:11:19.554
is just the talent that
you see on the screen.

00:11:19.554 --> 00:11:23.927
But at the same time, there
is a certain innocence,

00:11:23.927 --> 00:11:26.589
and yet a certain power.

00:11:26.589 --> 00:11:30.506
And it seems to me it's
both artist and also...

00:11:32.616 --> 00:11:33.882
business.

00:11:33.882 --> 00:11:34.938
- Aw thank you.

00:11:34.938 --> 00:11:36.259
- [Interviewer] But do you believe that?

00:11:36.259 --> 00:11:37.092
- Yeah.

00:11:37.092 --> 00:11:37.925
- [Interviewer] Am I right?

00:11:37.925 --> 00:11:38.758
- I've worked very hard (laughs)

00:11:38.758 --> 00:11:41.341
to make that true so thank you.

00:11:43.515 --> 00:11:44.348
- [Interviewer] That it's both that.

00:11:44.348 --> 00:11:46.552
- Thank you, well because without gaining

00:11:46.552 --> 00:11:47.846
some control over the business,

00:11:47.846 --> 00:11:51.490
I lose some control over the creative,

00:11:51.490 --> 00:11:52.879
which is most important.

00:11:52.879 --> 00:11:54.755
- [Interviewer] The more
you control the business,

00:11:54.755 --> 00:11:56.483
the more you can give
flower to the creative.

00:11:56.483 --> 00:11:57.343
- Exactly.

00:11:57.343 --> 00:11:59.095
- [Interviewer] Or give
wings to the creative.

00:11:59.095 --> 00:12:01.490
- Yeah so I used to stay out of it,

00:12:01.490 --> 00:12:03.403
I don't care, I don't
want to, I'm an artist.

00:12:03.403 --> 00:12:04.460
(laughs)

00:12:04.460 --> 00:12:05.409
I don't need to.

00:12:05.409 --> 00:12:07.286
You know, but this is my business now,

00:12:07.286 --> 00:12:09.135
it is important if you agent,

00:12:09.135 --> 00:12:10.455
my agent always says

00:12:10.455 --> 00:12:14.298
I'm his only client that ever
calls him back as soon as he,

00:12:14.298 --> 00:12:15.182
(laughing)

00:12:15.182 --> 00:12:16.735
Because it's my business
and I respect my business.

00:12:16.735 --> 00:12:17.672
- [Interviewer] Your agent
says you're the only client

00:12:17.672 --> 00:12:18.929
that calls him back to say,

00:12:18.929 --> 00:12:19.762
- Immediately.

00:12:19.762 --> 00:12:22.231
If I have a missed call, I
call, I'm probably the only.

00:12:22.231 --> 00:12:25.384
I return emails, I return
phone calls, because

00:12:25.384 --> 00:12:26.495
- [Interviewer] Because you want to know

00:12:26.495 --> 00:12:27.842
what options there are.

00:12:27.842 --> 00:12:29.928
- Yes, I've worked really
hard to build this,

00:12:29.928 --> 00:12:31.872
and I want to continue building it,

00:12:31.872 --> 00:12:34.090
and it's my business,
my personal business.

00:12:34.090 --> 00:12:37.098
So I don't understand how people do slack.

00:12:37.098 --> 00:12:40.157
- I have a disease, I'm a workaholic.

00:12:40.157 --> 00:12:44.338
It is like being an alcoholic,
I just can't give it up.

00:12:44.338 --> 00:12:48.699
I've been unwell for two
weeks, and I feel unwell,

00:12:48.699 --> 00:12:49.617
so I can't go to work.

00:12:49.617 --> 00:12:52.143
Because I can't go to work, I get worse.

00:12:52.143 --> 00:12:55.258
If I sit in one place I am stressed.

00:12:55.258 --> 00:12:57.556
The reason I am not fidgeting too much

00:12:57.556 --> 00:12:58.835
is because I am unwell,

00:12:58.835 --> 00:13:00.495
otherwise I hate sitting in one place.

00:13:00.495 --> 00:13:03.583
Some work has to be given to me,

00:13:03.583 --> 00:13:05.143
My wife and children keep telling me,

00:13:05.143 --> 00:13:08.075
"You know you work 20
years round the clock,

00:13:08.075 --> 00:13:09.263
"why are you doing this?"

00:13:09.263 --> 00:13:10.995
I don't think I need the money.

00:13:10.995 --> 00:13:13.595
I don't think I am greedy for more fame.

00:13:13.595 --> 00:13:15.180
I'm doing okay,

00:13:15.180 --> 00:13:17.413
I can do one film in two
years and be happy with it.

00:13:17.413 --> 00:13:18.655
But I'm just not happy.

00:13:18.655 --> 00:13:20.938
I tell everyone if I'm
not working I'm depressed.

00:13:20.938 --> 00:13:24.043
I don't know if it's
a curse or a blessing.

00:13:24.043 --> 00:13:26.103
Maybe at the end of it all when we die,

00:13:26.103 --> 00:13:27.435
god will say, "Did you work?"

00:13:27.435 --> 00:13:28.771
I'll say, "Yes a lot."

00:13:28.771 --> 00:13:30.129
"Oh damn, you go to hell

00:13:30.129 --> 00:13:31.993
"because I didn't want anyone to work."

00:13:31.993 --> 00:13:33.657
Or it could be, "Oh you worked,

00:13:33.657 --> 00:13:36.151
"that's really nice because
I wanted everyone to work."

00:13:36.151 --> 00:13:38.385
So I don't know, one of
them is going to be right.

00:13:38.385 --> 00:13:41.612
But I just keep working, I think,

00:13:41.612 --> 00:13:44.862
I have this fear that you
have very few days in life.

00:13:44.862 --> 00:13:47.267
And you need to cram them
with a lot of things.

00:13:47.267 --> 00:13:48.746
You need to fill it,

00:13:48.746 --> 00:13:50.516
fill it with so many things in there,

00:13:50.516 --> 00:13:52.033
that you don't have space for life.

00:13:52.033 --> 00:13:53.210
It should be like that.

00:13:53.210 --> 00:13:55.376
When I'm not working I get lonely,

00:13:55.376 --> 00:13:57.817
I get depressed, I get sad.

00:13:57.817 --> 00:14:00.790
I don't drink, I'm not a philosopher,

00:14:00.790 --> 00:14:02.402
I'm not a poet that I like loneliness.

00:14:02.402 --> 00:14:03.892
I hate being alone,

00:14:03.892 --> 00:14:05.794
and I hate being with people at times.

00:14:05.794 --> 00:14:07.959
I'm unsocial, I'm very odd with people.

00:14:07.959 --> 00:14:10.748
So what happens is I have replaced people

00:14:10.748 --> 00:14:12.080
with work in my life.

00:14:12.080 --> 00:14:13.716
Because when I am working
I am with a lot of people.

00:14:13.716 --> 00:14:15.766
And they are people I can interact with

00:14:15.766 --> 00:14:17.325
because somewhere there is a wall,

00:14:17.325 --> 00:14:19.158
that I am not interacting
with them socially,

00:14:19.158 --> 00:14:21.351
I am interacting with them to this common,

00:14:21.351 --> 00:14:24.547
you know, goal that is for work.

00:14:24.547 --> 00:14:26.897
So I can discuss with
her, or discuss with him.

00:14:26.897 --> 00:14:29.036
And appear friends for that moment.

00:14:29.036 --> 00:14:32.775
Many of my filmmaking colleagues complain

00:14:32.775 --> 00:14:34.768
that after the film is
over you don't talk to us.

00:14:34.768 --> 00:14:37.462
I can't explain to them I don't
have anything else to say.

00:14:37.462 --> 00:14:38.982
I love you,

00:14:38.982 --> 00:14:40.330
uh I miss you.

00:14:40.330 --> 00:14:42.997
But I miss you through the work.

00:14:44.803 --> 00:14:46.854
I'm very fond of a lot of people,

00:14:46.854 --> 00:14:48.688
a lot of people I love like family,

00:14:48.688 --> 00:14:51.581
and I can't pick up the
phone and talk with them,

00:14:51.581 --> 00:14:53.812
I can't socialize with them, I
can't sit and chat with them.

00:14:53.812 --> 00:14:56.466
But when we are working
I can just be with them.

00:14:56.466 --> 00:14:58.216
So I don't know what.

00:14:59.935 --> 00:15:01.630
It's all about,

00:15:01.630 --> 00:15:04.905
I have replaced loneliness,
depression, and people,

00:15:04.905 --> 00:15:05.950
with work.

00:15:05.950 --> 00:15:08.180
- When you come from the
working class background

00:15:08.180 --> 00:15:09.597
that I come from.

00:15:11.443 --> 00:15:12.776
I feel guilty...

00:15:16.487 --> 00:15:20.002
At ten to five, if I am not in the office.

00:15:20.002 --> 00:15:23.252
I know that might sound strange to you.

00:15:24.443 --> 00:15:26.860
But it's a work ethic that's,

00:15:27.703 --> 00:15:29.536
I have to be half dead

00:15:31.157 --> 00:15:32.657
not to go to work.

00:15:33.594 --> 00:15:37.954
It's kind of a work ethic
that's built into me.

00:15:37.954 --> 00:15:41.204
But nevertheless, having said all that,

00:15:42.454 --> 00:15:45.525
the weekends, that was it, it's over,

00:15:45.525 --> 00:15:46.461
switch off like a light.

00:15:46.461 --> 00:15:48.877
Friday night as you got
back from the office

00:15:48.877 --> 00:15:51.114
or from the work, or from
the factory, whatever,

00:15:51.114 --> 00:15:55.238
no matter what was buzzing
around in my head, it was off.

00:15:55.238 --> 00:15:56.742
Like a light switch.

00:15:56.742 --> 00:15:58.821
And that's it, don't let it interfere.

00:15:58.821 --> 00:16:00.550
Very rarely let it
interfere with the weekend.

00:16:00.550 --> 00:16:04.816
And then Monday morning,
bang, back on it again

00:16:04.816 --> 00:16:06.767
as if you flicked the switch on again.

00:16:06.767 --> 00:16:10.383
So I had a bit of discipline
in that respect, yeah.

00:16:10.383 --> 00:16:12.485
- Thank you guys so much for watching.

00:16:12.485 --> 00:16:13.533
I hope you enjoyed.

00:16:13.533 --> 00:16:14.447
I'd love to know,

00:16:14.447 --> 00:16:16.548
what was your biggest
takeaway from this video?

00:16:16.548 --> 00:16:18.641
What are you going to immediately apply

00:16:18.641 --> 00:16:20.501
somehow to your life or to your business?

00:16:20.501 --> 00:16:21.964
Leave it down in the comments below,

00:16:21.964 --> 00:16:23.314
I'm super curious to find out.

00:16:23.314 --> 00:16:24.856
So thank you guys again for watching.

00:16:24.856 --> 00:16:25.955
I believe in you.

00:16:25.955 --> 00:16:28.231
I hope you continue to believe in yourself

00:16:28.231 --> 00:16:30.045
and whatever your one word is.

00:16:30.045 --> 00:16:33.147
Much love, I'll see you soon.

00:16:33.147 --> 00:16:35.147
(woosh)

